gamespot originally reviewed warframe in  2013 and gave it a six out of ten due to  substantial revisions and new content  since its debut we have re-examined  warframe as it is in 2019 and produced a  new review to reflect its current state  [Music]  in 2013 a sci-fi Luke Bryan starring a  cast of space ninjas was an inspired  concept in our original review we  praised the fast paced gameplay but it  was clear that warframe lacked  meaningful features that took advantage  of its strengths now in 2019 warframe  has evolved it's much more than just  space ninjas brawling in corridors it  takes a decidedly unorthodox approach  with its nonlinear adventure  sometimes frustratingly so yet  journeying across warframes massive  universe is as satisfying as it is  endearing throughout the sprawling  campaign you slash shoot and free run  through different missions with an  impressive amount of freedom to take on  different objectives there's a  staggering amount of activities to dive  into and with well over 40 hyper  stylized frames to utilize there's a  constant sense of fun and surprise when  discovering how deep it all runs to play  warframe can often feel like playing a  larger game of catch-up with six years  of content but it's a game that  definitely rewards patience as most  missions are set on standalone levels  across the star chart the games  piecemeal structure ultimately makes the  massive game more digestible  despite its growth over the years  warframes roots are still firmly planted  in its fast-paced and satisfying combat  which is intuitive enough for newcomers  to dive into the overall speed and  flexibility in its action are excellent  and there's a constant sense of grace  that can make even the ordinary missions  thrilling giving way to the most  gratifying and stylish encounters the  true star of warframe are the various  frames with each one possessing its own  traits and abilities it always feels  rewarding when you find a new frame  especially ones that are highly  specialized these include the stealth  oriented ash the aquatic alien tentacle  summoning hydroid and the bard like  Octavia who can craft music to amplify  your abilities and attack enemies you  have free rein to experiment with unique  frames and it can be especially fun  strengthening them with powerful mods  and armaments that can melt through  enemies it's also impressive how  in-depth customization is in warframe  and it's rare to find another player who  has your same style and loadout while  the core combat is often satisfying it's  still common to see a streak of highly  repetitive missions most of which  recycle assets for procedurally  generated levels and objective types  this repetition can create a recurring  feeling of deja vu and there were times  when this left me feeling exhausted  after an extended session warframe does  try to inject  a number of variations to mix this up  though some missions Accor per 8 the use  of the archwing switching up the  familiar action sequences with deep  space shooter levels there's even a set  of PvP game types such as the Conclave  and duel modes with the latter letting  you invite another player to a clan dojo  to engage in a solo fight but when  compared to the PvE experience the  competitive activities come across as  exceedingly basic and clunky warframe  takes a mostly hands-off approach in  getting you in the rhythm of cutting  down mobs of enemies and boosting your  frames strengths by collecting mods  however this can sometimes create  feelings of aimlessness which is  magnified when it becomes apparent that  there isn't a traditional end game to  work up to warframe is a challenging  game to crack if you're committed to  figuring out the intricacies of the game  then using online guides to understand  its in-depth mechanics as a must these  resources are a big help but is  disappointing how often yet to use them  as opposed to the game itself teaching  you the same information without them  learning these systems on your own can  be a significant test of patience  fortunately warframe does have an active  and open community to seek assistance  from and you can directly interact with  others at various social spaces across  different planets stick with the game  long enough and you'll unlock access to  the more involved cinematic story  missions and open-world settings which  left me wanting to invest more time to  continue interacting with warframes  characters in the plains of idolan and  the orb palace you can take in the  sights at the large-scale worlds engage  in dynamic bounties and events with  squads and even learn more about the  brewing conflict within each setting the  valises stories especially engaging  dealing with workers rights and the  perils of late-stage capitalism in the  interstellar age I'm continually pleased  with the flexibility of warframes many  systems and how it allows for you to  attain a variety of rewards at your own  pace of course there's an assortment of  items weapons and even frames to  purchase with real money or platinum  warframes premium currency fortunately  most items in the game are attainable  through gameplay allowing you to get  into the nitty-gritty of the games  content mostly unabated the economy of  warframe is very active and if you're  resourceful enough you can even trade  some of your own gear and blueprints  with other players for platinum as well  when new content is introduced the  pathway to experience in the quest or  acquiring the next frame is available to  all players this relaxed approach is  reassuring especially for a game of this  magnitude and I generally found  acquiring gear and new classes to be  quite manageable however there's still  some time sinks that feel mostly  arbitrary resulting in the expected and  sometimes lengthy grind that's  commonplace in free-to-play games to  that end the primary intent of platinum  is to circumvent both investments of  time and resources  it's interesting how much warframe is  improved yet also how much has stayed  the same over the years though it still  has issues with repetition and lack of  explanations for its more complex  systems it's managed to overcome their  severity by introducing so many events  and revisions that continue to elevate  the game  there are some expected bouts of  frustration to be found but I always  manage to Center myself once I move on  to other opportunities in a lot of ways  that's what warframe manages to do best  just when you feel like you've hit a  lull a better and more fulfilling  opportunity will likely present itself  perhaps most importantly warframe makes  sure that the time spent in its world is  almost always well rewarded  you
 

